        "slideId": "intake-p2",
        "blockId": "b3",
        "severity": "error",
        "problem": "The qualification that the headline depends on — \"Two teams, eight weeks. Quality and error rates were not measured. [1][2]\" — is set at 25px light-gray caption weight tucked under the chart, roughly a third the size of the 50px bold claim \"Review time fell from 40 to 16 hours [1]\". Since the deck's decision turns on this not-measured limitation, presenting it as a footnote-level caption gives it misleadingly weak emphasis relative to the claim it restricts.",
        "correction": "Raise this line to body emphasis (about 29–32px, darker text) and place it directly beneath the headline as a stated qualifier, or give it its own tinted band under the chart, keeping the wording \"Two teams, eight weeks. Quality and error rates were not measured. [1][2]\" and its markers exactly as-is.",
        "route": "layout"
      },
      {
        "slideId": "intake-p2",
        "blockId": "b2",
        "severity": "advice",
        "problem": "The chart region shows two stacked label lines saying the same thing — the 29px subtitle \"Median review time, hours. [1]\" and immediately below it the 22px axis title \"Median (hours)\" — which adds clutter and competes with the headline without adding information.",
        "correction": "Suppress the chart's axis-title style in the scene so only the subtitle \"Median review time, hours. [1]\" carries the unit; no wording on the slide itself needs to change.",
        "route": "layout"
      },
      {
        "slideId": "intake-p2",
        "blockId": "b2",
        "severity": "advice",
        "problem": "Faint vertical gridlines are only partially rendered: they do not appear across the full plot height, are cut off at the gray bar's lower edge, and one appears to run over the blue \"During the pilot\" bar. The result reads as a half-drawn chart rather than deliberate gridlines.",
        "correction": "Either hide the vertical gridlines entirely or render them consistently behind both bars across the full plot area at a single low-contrast weight, so no line stops mid-chart or crosses a bar.",
        "route": "layout"
      }
    ],
